const takeaway = `
<p>Both approaches read the array once, so this pair is not a lesson about time.
It is a lesson about <strong>space</strong>. The tally keeps a row per distinct value —
around <code>n/2</code> rows here — and the statement's "use only constant extra space"
clause is what rules it out. That clause is in the body of the problem, not a follow-up,
which is the only reason the second approach is worth the cleverness.</p>

<p>What XOR actually gives you is three properties. It is <strong>associative</strong> and
<strong>commutative</strong>, so the order you fold the array in cannot change the result —
you may as well pretend the pairs are adjacent. <code>x ^ x == 0</code>, so a pair erases
itself. <code>x ^ 0 == x</code>, so the accumulator can start empty and the lone value
survives untouched. Fold the whole array and every column of the result answers one
question: did an odd number of values set this bit? Only the unpaired value did.</p>

<p>That identity is the trick behind a small family: <strong>Missing Number</strong>
(XOR the values against the indices and the gap survives), <strong>Single Number III</strong>
(fold everything, then split the array on any set bit of the result to separate the two
loners), and finding a swapped or duplicated pair between two collections that should
have matched.</p>

<p>Be clear with yourself that it is a trick. The counting version is what most people
should write first — it is obvious, it survives a change to the premise (three copies
instead of two, say) and it generalises to "which values appear an odd number of times".
The XOR version does exactly one thing, and it does it in two locals. Know it because
the constraint asks for it, not because it is shorter.</p>

<p>One detail the stage makes visible: XOR never counts anything. It has no idea whether
it has seen <code>2</code> twice or forty times — only whether the total is odd. Feed it
an array that breaks the premise and it returns a number with no meaning attached,
which is why the input control here refuses such an array rather than drawing one.</p>`;
